      "verdict": "reported",
      "measured": [
       0.0010197162129779282,
       0.01631545940764685
      ]
     }
    ],
    "froude": "Equal Froude number is a NECESSARY condition for two animals to move in a dynamically similar way, and later work is explicit that it is not sufficient -- cursorial and non-cursorial mammals do not move similarly at equal Fr. The widely quoted walk-to-run transition at Fr = 0.5 is a human result. Turning any of that into a pass or a fail for an arbitrary clade would be borrowing a threshold across exactly the gap this repository keeps finding and removing, so this check reports the number and stops."
   }
  },
  {
   "seq": 9,
   "type": "finding",
   "ts": "2026-11-03T06:32:31.013Z",
   "author": "agent:long-lived-fly",
   "body": "PARTLY EVALUATED: 1 of the 2 checks could not be run at all -- lift_carries_the_weight (the question asked is about POWERED flight, and this check cannot answer it. It compares lift with weight at a given airspeed, which is an area question; powered flight is a power question -- whether the flight muscle can supply the induced and profile power through a wingbeat, continuously, at that animal's mass. That needs a coefficient measured through a wingbeat and a muscle power density for the clade, and this repository has neither.). The checks that did run found no contradiction, and a check that did not run is not a check that passed. The flight branch refuses, and the refusal is the right one: the question asked is about POWERED flight, and this check cannot answer it. It compares lift with weight at a given airspeed, which is an area question; powered flight is a power question -- whether the flight muscle can supply the induced and profile power through a wingbeat, continuously, at that animal's mass. That needs a coefficient measured through a wingbeat and a muscle power density for the clade, and this repository has neither.",
   "lawset_version": "1.8.1",
   "tool": "locomotion_check (fly)",
   "verdict": "partly-evaluated",
   "generation": 2,
   "detail": {
    "verdict": "partly-evaluated",
    "lift": {
     "verdict": "cannot-evaluate",
     "limiting_factor": "the question asked is about POWERED flight, and this check cannot answer it. It compares lift with weight at a given airspeed, which is an area question; powered flight is a power question -- whether the flight muscle can supply the induced and profile power through a wingbeat, continuously, at that animal's mass. That needs a coefficient measured through a wingbeat and a muscle power density for the clade, and this repository has neither."
    },
    "why_this_is_correct": "The repository holds exactly two measured maximum lift coefficients: a stuffed Cypselurus hiraii and a live Petaurus breviceps. An insect at Re of order 100 with a flapping wing is aerodynamically unlike both -- unsteady, with leading-edge vortex lift that a steady coefficient does not represent at all. Returning a number here by borrowing one of the two would be exactly the failure this layer was built to stop."
   }
  },
  {
   "seq": 10,
   "type": "comment",
   "ts": "2026-11-03T06:32:31.015Z",
   "author": "agent:long-lived-fly",
   "body": "generation 3: Ecology is the one layer with any purchase here, and only sideways: a longer-lived fly is a fly exposed to predation for longer. The databases cannot tell us the lifespan, but they can tell us what is on record as eating this animal and whether any of it is in the place we are asking about.",
   "lawset_version": "1.8.1"
  },
  {
